{
 "spec": "1198-etl-sql-source",
 "note": "Each entry names the revision it was recorded at. The postgres-read-committed mutation did not bite: in PostgreSQL a single SELECT already reads one snapshot under READ COMMITTED, so the read's point in time does not depend on the level there; REPEATABLE READ is kept for any future multi-statement read. On SQL Server only the snapshot-disabled database test bites the isolation level: the mid-read change test also passes under READ COMMITTED, so it proves that writers are not blocked and a change after the read never reaches the run, not the isolation level itself. Review round 1 adds 8 entries recorded at f606d68cc. The date mutation bites only on SQL Server: Npgsql returns DateOnly for a date column, which another branch writes, while SqlClient returns DateTime. Review round 2 adds 3 entries recorded at 2fee631a8 (the interval refusal, the time-of-day format, which bites on SQL Server only because Npgsql returns TimeOnly, and the SQL column limit) and re-records source-table-identifier-unchecked at that revision so it lists all three failing theory rows. Review round 3 adds 2 entries that bite, recorded at 1cc03a3d4 (the 8-column key bound and the 63-character identifier bound), and 2 that do not, recorded on ce00cc26d with the round 3 changes not yet committed (they were committed unchanged as 1cc03a3d4): closing a cancelled reader did not throw on either driver at 200,000 rows, so the guard that keeps the original failure, and the cancel itself, are defensive. Review round 4 adds 1 entry, recorded at 9bb6fb935: the refusal of a zoned time of day. Review round 5 adds 3 entries, recorded at 6b1c8b1c6: the transformer's and output processor's SQL source branches, and the refusal of infinite dates. Review round 6 adds 2 entries, recorded at 7f7b177d7: extreme dates refused only where the engine maps infinity to them, and a zoned time of day with a precision. Review round 9 adds 1 entry, recorded at b6a886089: the worker's re-check that key columns are required. Review round 9 also adds 1 entry recorded at 23f8b1855: PostgreSQL locks the table before taking the snapshot, so a read behind a truncate-and-reload is not empty. Review round 10 adds 1 entry, recorded at 42572d6af: the read falls back to no table lock where PostgreSQL refuses it. Review round 11 adds 2 entries: at 63408195e, the fallback only for a lock the engine refused (a timed-out lock does not fall back), and at 63408195e, the catch that maps a value the driver cannot materialise to etl.sql-column-type-unsupported.",
